welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Stewart Schwartz the George Burton  Adams professor of history at Yale  University professor Schwartz  specializes in the history of colonial  Latin America especially Brazil and on  the history of early modern expansion  among his books our sovereignty and  society in colonial Brazil early Latin  America sugar plantations in the  formation of Brazilian society slaves  peasants and rebels Cambridge history of  native peoples of the Americas and South  America he is presently working on  several projects a history of  independence of Portugal and the crisis  of Iberian Atlantic 1622 1670 and a  social history of Caribbean hurricanes  today however we'll speak with him about  his newest groundbreaking book all can  be saved religious tolerance and  salvation in the iberian atlantic world  so let's begin with an overview of your  book and the premise well this is a book  about the history of tolerance not  toleration the way it's usually  understood by which people usually mean  state policy regarding religious freedom  but tolerance the attitude of people  towards the religion of others of their  neighbors and in that history usually  Spain and the Spanish world is left out  because it enters in only as an example  of intolerance the age of the  Inquisition  the suppression of religious freedom so  the book is somewhat distinct in that it  emphasizes Spain  and the origins of religious tolerance  in Spain and the spanish-speaking world  ok and what was your inspiration for  writing the book the inspiration came  one cold winter evening when I was  living in Minneapolis  I was reading a book by an Italian  scholar Carlo Ginsburg called the cheese  and the worms it's about the the  worldview of a very common man a Miller  and when he was being questioned by the  inquisitors he said to them well of  course he was a Catholic but if he had  been brought up in amongst the Turks he  would have thought their religion was  the right religion and he would have  found his salvation because after all  God loves us all and I thought at the  time gee that's a curious thing to say  for a man in the sixteenth century where  do they get those ideas  doesn't that sound quite unlike what  people ought to be thinking in the  sixteenth century I shut off the light  and went to sleep and forgot all about  it 20 years later I was working in the  Spanish archives and began to find  indications of other people who thought  the exactly the same thing and it struck  me as very curious how could that be  possible in the age of the Inquisition  that people would develop that kind of  idea of toleration and the idea that  salvation was possible in many religions  and that was the origins of the book  okay let's talk about your methodology a  bit you drew on an enormous body of  historical evidence to write your book  tell us a little bit about the process  and did you encounter any difficulties  well the Inquisition was a great  bureaucratic institution and it was  particularly interested in what people  not only what people did but what they  said what they thought and so it took  very very careful records and while not  all of those records have survived many  of them have for the Portuguese  Inquisition we have over 40,000 cases  Wow for the Spanish Inquisition also  about 40,000 and so it's possible to  reconstruct these these cases and these  are some of the few instances when  common people were recorded what they  actually thought so rather than being a  study of intellectuals it's possible to  do it a study of common people  that's the great advantage that the  Inquisition gives us it's sort of a  almost a kind of contradiction that we  owe so much to the life of and to the  ideas of common people that through this  basically ecclesiastical institution but  that's really the result of of this  investigation okay and your work really  differs from much of the existing work  for this period talk about that a little  bit well the way that toleration  religious toleration is usually  explained or thought of is in the terms  of great ideas  there's Erasmus or Spinoza or Rousseau  or John Locke and they write a kind of  breakthrough Brook or essay and then  somehow that catches on and lo and  behold you get to the modern world and  religious toleration becomes accepted  but the traditional way to look at that  is never to pay much attention at the  reception of these ideas how did the  idea get from Locke's essay on religious  tolerance how did it get to become a  general accepted idea no one ever pays  much attention to that so what makes my  book different is that it's interested  in the common people their ideas their  reception and the interaction of their  ideas would these great thinkers in fact  the book goes beyond that to suggest  that perhaps some of these ideas don't  come from the great thinkers down to the  people but in fact go the other way  around that these ideas are already  around and it's the great thinkers  synthesize them and integrate them into  an argument even though these ideas  aren't necessarily generated by them so  it's an inversion of the way in which we  usually think of intellectual change in  and what conclusions do you reach in  your book well the conclusion is that  one might argue that Spain had a  peculiar history because in the Middle  Ages there were Muslims and Jews and  Christians who lived together in Spain  and even though they didn't always get  along together nevertheless there was a  certain everyday interchange and they  understood each other at least even  though they might not always like each  other and when the Spanish go to the new  world they encounter Indians and then  later bring in Africans and so there was  already a kind of predisposition to  accept other cultures other ways of  thinking but one of the conclusions of  the book is that this this isn't a  peculiarly Spanish phenomenon and then  if you scratch deep enough in England or  Italy or France or Germany that you'll  find the same ideas it's just that the  way in which we've written the history  of the early modern world has basically  basically been in terms of the struggle  of religion so nobody's looked much for  cooperation or tolerance they've always  been interested in what drove people  apart not what drew them together but if  you scratch the surface a little bit you  can find lots of examples in Italy or  England or or in France and so in that  sense the book even though it's about  Spain and in and port Spain Portugal and  the Iberian Atlantic world that is the  Americas it concludes that in fact this  is a more general European phenomenon  and that we really need need to rethink  the whole idea of how toleration was  born and became an aspect of the modern  world fascinating and last question what  was one of the most surprising things  you found in doing your research well I  think the willingness of people to  contest Authority very simple people  people with hardly any education we're  at some point came to a personal  conviction that the church was wrong  on this issue the church's position we  have to remember was there is no  salvation outside the church you had to  be a member of the church in order to be  saved but common people with their own  sort of common sense couldn't quite get  it they didn't understand how could an  all merciful God condemn so many people  to eternal damnation it didn't make  sense to them and so they sometimes even  try to convince the inquisitors that the  inquisitors are wrong what's the most  curious about this is that these people  were willing to stand up against the  authority of the state and the authority  of the church in order to make this  argument they had no personal gain in  this it wasn't that it was good for  business  because by this time there were no Jews  in in Spain there were no Muslims left  in Spain so there was no direct economic  or personal benefit but these people  became convinced out of their belief  some of them were skeptics some of them  were atheist but some of them thought of  themselves as good Catholics and simply  thought on this particular issue that  the church had got it wrong in some way  these people are closer to the position  of the Roman Catholic Church after  vatican ii in the 20th century then the  inquisitors were the questions that were  trying to correct them were to vatican  ii and so the most interesting thing I  think in is that we have to listen to  the voice of these common people and the  fact that they're uneducated doesn't  mean that they don't have a common sense  understanding of the world and a desire  to live in a better world a world that  they could foresee one in which it  didn't matter so much what church their  neighbor went to so long as it was a  good neighbor very good thank you so  much for being here with us today and  sharing some of your work it's been a  pleasure for more information about  professor Schwartz and his work please  visit our website at yale.edu backslash  Macmillan report be sure to join us  again  another episode of the Macmillan report  made possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Centre for  International and area studies at Yale
 

